The '''2010 South Carolina gubernatorial election''' took place on November 2, 2010. Incumbent [[Republican Party (United States)|Republican]] [[Governor of South Carolina|Governor]] [[Mark Sanford]] was [[term limits in the United States|term-limited]] and unable to seek re-election. [[Partisan primary|Primary election]]s took place on June 8, 2010, and a [[Two-round system|runoff election]], as was necessary on the Republican side, was held two weeks later on June 22.

Republican [[Nikki Haley]] defeated Democrat [[Vincent Sheheen]] in the general election by a margin of 4.5%. As of 2023, this is the closest that the Democrats have come to winning the governorship of South Carolina since their last victory in [[1998 South Carolina gubernatorial election|1998]]. This is the first open-seat election since [[1994 South Carolina gubernatorial election|1994]]. Haley was re-elected in [[2014 South Carolina gubernatorial election|2014]] in a rematch with Sheheen.

==Republican primary==
According to [[CNN]], Haley initially entered the gubernatorial primary as a [[dark horse]] candidate. In an article covering her surge in the primary in the weeks prior to the election, it was noted that a "surprise" endorsement from former Alaska governor and [[2008 United States presidential election|2008]] vice presidential candidate [[Sarah Palin]] boosted Haley's candidacy. Haley's campaign was backed by TV ads run by ReformSC, an advocacy group funded by allies of outgoing Governor [[Mark Sanford]].<ref>{{Cite web |last=Hamby |first=Peter |date=May 20, 2010 |title=Haley has momentum in South Carolina race, rivals admit |url=https://politicalticker.blogs.cnn.com/2010/05/20/haley-has-momentum-in-south-carolina-race-rivals-admit/ |access-date=February 8, 2023 |website=CNN |language=en |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20100523235726/https://politicalticker.blogs.cnn.com/2010/05/20/haley-has-momentum-in-south-carolina-race-rivals-admit/ |archive-date=May 23, 2010 |url-status=dead}}</ref>

